WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.080
Good morning, John.

00:00:01.080 --> 00:00:04.919
I just recently, along with most Americans,
wrote the government a big fat check, because

00:00:04.919 --> 00:00:09.710
that’s what life is. Uh, nothing is certain
except for death and taxes. Uh, and also the

00:00:09.710 --> 00:00:12.200
speed of light in a vacuum, and actually,
now that I think about it, many universal

00:00:12.200 --> 00:00:15.570
constants are more certain than death and
taxes, but I’m getting ahead of myself.

00:00:15.570 --> 00:00:22.100
Taxes! Ah, while I’m not precisely happy
to be rid of my money, I am in favor of government

00:00:22.100 --> 00:00:27.390
and all that jazz. And most of the 16,000
freakin' dollars that I just sent to the US

00:00:27.390 --> 00:00:30.160
government is going to be used to decrease
suck in some way: making sure that people

00:00:30.160 --> 00:00:33.470
who lose their jobs don’t become immediately
homeless, education for people who wouldn’t

00:00:33.470 --> 00:00:36.870
otherwise be educated, and healthcare for
people who otherwise couldn’t afford it.

00:00:36.870 --> 00:00:40.261
All of these fantastic things! But when it
comes right down to it, not a lot of the money

00:00:40.261 --> 00:00:44.050
that I just gave my government is going to
be used to do cool stuff. Don’t get me wrong,

00:00:44.050 --> 00:00:47.890
I’m totally in favor of decreasing suck,
but like my grandfather always said, “using

00:00:47.890 --> 00:00:52.821
the awesome power of the United States government
to decrease suck without ever increasing awesome

00:00:52.821 --> 00:00:56.770
is a little bit like using the Starship Enterprise
as an interstellar shipping barge.”

00:00:56.770 --> 00:01:00.850
But for people like me, I am happy to announce
that deep in the budget there are a few percentage

00:01:00.850 --> 00:01:04.530
points dedicated to increasing awesome. For
example, the money that the government spends

00:01:04.530 --> 00:01:07.980
on science and technology. This is a graphical
representation of the United States budget.

00:01:07.980 --> 00:01:12.030
It’s divided up, and you might think: this,
this, this, maybe some of these things are

00:01:12.030 --> 00:01:15.850
the science, no. No, the science is down - it's
that little thing down there! And less than

00:01:15.850 --> 00:01:19.670
half of that is my very favorite government
agency, NASA.

00:01:19.670 --> 00:01:23.460
Now if I magically shrunk the United States
budget into a dollar and then split that dollar

00:01:23.460 --> 00:01:26.900
into a hundred pennies, I still could not
show you NASA’s relative budget because

00:01:26.900 --> 00:01:31.760
it would be smaller than one half of a penny.
But despite that fact, it still is a relatively

00:01:31.760 --> 00:01:36.650
large amount of money, about 18 billion dollars,
and this begs the question, "What is NASA

00:01:36.650 --> 00:01:39.370
doing with all that money?"

00:01:39.370 --> 00:01:43.760
Besides maintaining a five-bedroom house in
outer space that has been constantly inhabited

00:01:43.760 --> 00:01:47.430
for over ten years, a space station that provides
our first permanent foot hold in space and

00:01:47.430 --> 00:01:53.659
a literal and scientific and metaphorical
stepping off point into the exploration of

00:01:53.659 --> 00:01:57.210
the rest of the solar system. A place where
so much science is done that I literally cannot

00:01:57.210 --> 00:02:00.690
even show you the names of all of the studies
that have been done there.

00:02:00.690 --> 00:02:04.150
Besides launching what amounts to a pretty
much remote-controlled SUV onto the surface

00:02:04.150 --> 00:02:08.740
of Mars with ten times the scientific payload
of any interplanetary mission so far. A remote

00:02:08.740 --> 00:02:12.560
controlled laboratory that will answer questions
about Mars, including whether it once harbored

00:02:12.560 --> 00:02:16.380
life, and help prepare us for manned exploration
of the Red Planet.

00:02:16.380 --> 00:02:19.600
Besides creating a worthy successor to the
Hubble Space Telescope, allowing astronomers

00:02:19.600 --> 00:02:23.920
to see further than it had ever hoped to see
before and allowing humanity a first glimpse

00:02:23.920 --> 00:02:30.840
at the very origins of the universe. A telescope
that can see 13.4 billion years into the past.

00:02:30.840 --> 00:02:35.410
Besides inviting the private sector into the
space industry, offering them patents on technologies

00:02:35.410 --> 00:02:39.400
developed for NASA and fostering the growth
of an entirely new sector of the economy.

00:02:39.400 --> 00:02:42.270
Besides placing the first artificial satellite
around Mercury.

00:02:42.270 --> 00:02:46.260
Besides making air travel faster and safer
and more environmentally friendly.

00:02:46.260 --> 00:02:50.260
Besides building the first telescope capable
of capturing high resolution X-ray images.

00:02:50.260 --> 00:02:53.780
Besides adding three more climate monitoring
satellites to the already existing fleet that

00:02:53.780 --> 00:02:57.740
will help us understand our changing climate
and how to react to global climate change.

00:02:57.740 --> 00:03:01.209
Besides creating frameworks and technologies
and challenges that will help inspire the

00:03:01.209 --> 00:03:03.780
next generation of engineers and mathematicians
and scientists.

00:03:03.780 --> 00:03:08.130
Besides identifying thousands of planets outside
of our solar system, many of them apparently

00:03:08.130 --> 00:03:09.470
Earth-like, with liquid water.

00:03:09.470 --> 00:03:13.130
Besides watching the final two Space Shuttle
missions while simultaneously developing a

00:03:13.130 --> 00:03:16.410
new multipurpose crew vehicle, capable of
visiting not just the orbit of the Earth,

00:03:16.410 --> 00:03:19.300
but possibly the Moon and Mars and Venus and
beyond.

00:03:19.300 --> 00:03:23.790
And besides being the best chance that we
have of answering fundamental questions about

00:03:23.790 --> 00:03:25.970
ourselves and our place in the universe.

00:03:25.970 --> 00:03:30.700
Besides that, I guess NASA doesn’t do much
with that 0.45% of our tax dollars.

00:03:30.700 --> 00:03:36.580
I’m happy to give my money to NASA because
I want my government to be doing awesome things.

00:03:36.580 --> 00:03:39.930
I believe in my country's ability to be awesome.
I’m afraid that more and more we’re just

00:03:39.930 --> 00:03:44.750
focusing on maintenance and not on the amazing
things that we can do when we combine our

00:03:44.750 --> 00:03:47.459
efforts, and that, really, is what government
is about.

00:03:47.459 --> 00:03:51.400
So thank you to NASA for doing such great
awesome and exciting with our money. It’s

00:03:51.400 --> 00:03:55.269
strange to say this, but I can almost not
wait to give you my money again in 2012.

00:03:55.269 --> 00:03:56.330
John, I’ll see you on Monday.

